WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:01.170
- In 2018,

00:00:01.170 --> 00:00:04.050
the Federal Reserve Bank of
St. Louis published a study,

00:00:04.050 --> 00:00:05.430
which found that households

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.030
headed by people born in
the '80s, AKA millennials,

00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:10.710
had accumulated less wealth

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:13.470
than previous generations
had at a similar age,

00:00:13.470 --> 00:00:16.170
34% less on average.

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.040
Yikes.

00:00:17.040 --> 00:00:18.180
- Over the next few years,

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:21.210
there were a torrent
of articles and videos,

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.680
including one of ours,

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:26.670
examining how and why this
so-called lost generation

00:00:26.670 --> 00:00:29.730
ended up with such a bum economic deal.

00:00:29.730 --> 00:00:32.070
Were they screwed over by the boomers

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:34.740
or an addiction to avocado toast?

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:38.670
- Then, in 2021, the same
researchers took another look

00:00:38.670 --> 00:00:40.620
at the millennial financial forecast

00:00:40.620 --> 00:00:42.540
with new methods and fresh data,

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.520
and found something surprising.

00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:46.860
Somehow, over the last few years,

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.650
millennials had made
remarkable financial gains.

00:00:49.650 --> 00:00:52.800
- Bae, does that mean we
can eat real Tide pods again

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.690
instead of the generic brand?

00:00:54.690 --> 00:00:58.170
Can the lost generation
finally start adulting?

00:00:58.170 --> 00:01:01.650
- The data is a bit more
nuanced than lost or found,

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:03.450
but maybe, just maybe,

00:01:03.450 --> 00:01:07.230
we can call a truce in the
intergenerational warfare.

00:01:07.230 --> 00:01:08.063
- Cool beans.

00:01:08.063 --> 00:01:10.640
(bright music)

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:19.470
The 2016 data looked pretty
grim for millennials.

00:01:19.470 --> 00:01:22.020
Not only had they accumulated less wealth

00:01:22.020 --> 00:01:25.590
than Gen Xers and boomers at
similar points in their lives,

00:01:25.590 --> 00:01:27.540
they were earning less income,

00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.640
had a lower rate of home ownership,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:31.500
and possibly as a result,

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:35.760
were delaying big life milestones
like marriage and kids.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:37.500
- A lot of factors were suggested.

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:39.540
The soaring cost of college tuition

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:41.100
had saddled the generation

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:43.440
with unprecedented student debt,

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:45.390
rising healthcare costs made it difficult

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:48.000
to save for retirement or
build an emergency fund,

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:50.100
and a chronic housing shortage

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:53.190
kept home prices too high
for millennials to afford.

00:01:53.190 --> 00:01:54.300
- [Julia] But the big reason

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.120
was the Great Recession of 2008.

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:00.180
Many millennials were just
entering the workforce

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:03.240
when the economic landscape
was at its bleakest state

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:04.830
since the Great Depression.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.780
The unemployment rate for young people

00:02:06.780 --> 00:02:09.990
was substantially higher
than other age groups,

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.420
meaning many millennials had no choice

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.550
but to stay at their parents' homes

00:02:14.550 --> 00:02:16.620
using whatever income they could get

00:02:16.620 --> 00:02:19.650
to try and pay down big student loans.

00:02:19.650 --> 00:02:22.620
The one thing they weren't
doing was building wealth,

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.140
a handicap that persisted for years.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:27.120
- So, what turned things around?

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:28.290
Well, it took a while,

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:30.750
but basically the economy improved.

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:32.190
Starting in the mid-2010s,

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:34.260
unemployment fell, businesses recovered,

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.320
and the demand for young
professional labor rose.

00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:39.660
By 2019, the median millennial household

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:42.270
was making around $10,000 more a year

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:44.940
than previous generations
did at the same age.

00:02:44.940 --> 00:02:47.250
And yes, that's adjusted for inflation.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.100
- Wealth accumulation
is still lagging a bit,

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:55.100
but the gap has closed
significantly, from 34% to only 11%,

00:02:56.160 --> 00:02:59.610
as millennials finally start
paying down their student loans

00:02:59.610 --> 00:03:02.610
and investing money into
stocks and real estate.

00:03:02.610 --> 00:03:06.600
Home ownership rates from
millennials are now at 52%,

00:03:06.600 --> 00:03:11.600
still below, but not far from
boomers 57% at the same age.

00:03:12.060 --> 00:03:14.160
It's almost like a rubber band effect,

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:15.990
where the millennials were held back

00:03:15.990 --> 00:03:17.550
during the Great Recession,

00:03:17.550 --> 00:03:20.403
but then catapulted ahead
by the Great Recovery.

00:03:21.390 --> 00:03:23.550
- Education has a lot to do with that.

00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:25.470
Millennials are much more likely

00:03:25.470 --> 00:03:29.130
to have a four-year college
degree than previous generations

00:03:29.130 --> 00:03:31.080
which not only burdens them with debt,

00:03:31.080 --> 00:03:33.960
but also delays entry into the workforce.

00:03:33.960 --> 00:03:36.030
But, as we've said in previous videos,

00:03:36.030 --> 00:03:38.850
that degree does pay off later in life.

00:03:38.850 --> 00:03:41.220
Now, millennials are in
their peak earning years

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:44.370
at a time when the labor market
is pretty favorable to them.

00:03:44.370 --> 00:03:45.420
- Skrt!

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:47.130
Those numbers are totes on fleek,

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:49.320
but there's something sus about it.

00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:52.980
If things are so lit, then
why is the vibe so off?

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:54.810
Is the struggle real?

00:03:54.810 --> 00:03:55.643
- It's true.

00:03:55.643 --> 00:03:56.820
Despite the positive data,

00:03:56.820 --> 00:03:59.520
millennials are still feeling a bit salty,

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.100
at least if the platform
formally known as Twitter

00:04:02.100 --> 00:04:03.810
is an accurate representation.

00:04:03.810 --> 00:04:06.960
- Psychologist Jean Twenge hypothesizes

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:11.310
that FOMO may play a part in
this generational negativity.

00:04:11.310 --> 00:04:14.310
Humans don't assess
their status in a vacuum,

00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:16.560
but in relation to others.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:17.580
If you perceive

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:19.920
that your neighbors are
doing better than you,

00:04:19.920 --> 00:04:21.000
you may experience

00:04:21.000 --> 00:04:24.570
what psychologists call
relative deprivation.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:26.970
- Now, that's been going
on since the dawn of man,

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.920
but thanks to social media,

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:31.380
millennials may be the
first generation exposed

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:35.520
to an endless slideshow of
wealth, luxury, and privilege,

00:04:35.520 --> 00:04:38.640
constantly reminding them
of what they don't have.

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:40.950
Coupled with the general
trend of news headlines

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:42.150
on the right and the left,

00:04:42.150 --> 00:04:44.940
getting more angry,
pessimistic, and fearful,

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:47.730
this may have created a
sense of economic despair

00:04:47.730 --> 00:04:48.990
that's hard to shake.

00:04:48.990 --> 00:04:51.420
- This may seem like a dismissive take,

00:04:51.420 --> 00:04:53.580
but it's not like other generations

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:55.830
didn't have their share of hardships.

00:04:55.830 --> 00:04:59.670
Many Gen Xers also entered the
workforce during a recession,

00:04:59.670 --> 00:05:02.520
had a higher rate of
poverty than millennials,

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:04.590
and lost a ton of wealth

00:05:04.590 --> 00:05:07.680
when the housing bubble burst in 2007.

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:10.470
And if you think this
bout of inflation is bad,

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:13.020
you don't wanna know what
boomers had to live through

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:14.130
in the '70s.

00:05:14.130 --> 00:05:16.860
- This is not to say that
all millennials have it easy.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:20.070
We've been using words
like median and average

00:05:20.070 --> 00:05:23.670
to describe a generally
positive economic trend,

00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.850
but that may obscure a
concerning financial divide

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:28.890
within the millennial generation,

00:05:28.890 --> 00:05:31.740
and the main fault line
seems to be education.

00:05:31.740 --> 00:05:33.720
- While millennials
with a four-year degree

00:05:33.720 --> 00:05:36.840
have enjoyed the benefits
of a rising economy,

00:05:36.840 --> 00:05:39.060
those with only a high school diploma

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:43.170
have actually lost ground
compared to previous generations.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:45.300
The same goes for millennial men,

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.570
probably because of their lower
rates of college attendance

00:05:48.570 --> 00:05:50.550
relative to millennial women.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:53.910
With many women now outearning
their male partners,

00:05:53.910 --> 00:05:55.470
it starts to make sense

00:05:55.470 --> 00:05:58.350
why millennial couples
are delaying parenthood.

00:05:58.350 --> 00:06:00.330
- Another group that is not enjoying

00:06:00.330 --> 00:06:02.940
a proportional share of
the generational recovery

00:06:02.940 --> 00:06:04.290
is Black millennials

00:06:04.290 --> 00:06:06.150
who, while making slightly more

00:06:06.150 --> 00:06:09.360
than black Gen Xers and
boomers did at a similar age,

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:12.390
are a staggering 50% behind

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:14.550
when it comes to accumulating wealth.

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:16.860
Much of this can be
attributed to student debt,

00:06:16.860 --> 00:06:19.650
which tends to be much
higher for Black graduates.

00:06:19.650 --> 00:06:21.540
- [Julia] And if you're
one of those millennials

00:06:21.540 --> 00:06:22.950
who managed to buy a house

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:26.190
before home prices and
interest rates shot up,

00:06:26.190 --> 00:06:28.650
you're probably sitting pretty right now.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:29.490
If not,

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:33.300
this ongoing housing shortage
is a true financial nightmare.

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:36.120
- What all this suggests is
that despite the headlines,

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:38.730
the real economic dividing line in America

00:06:38.730 --> 00:06:42.090
isn't about generation,
but generational wealth.

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.130
- If your parents helped you go to college

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:47.460
without taking on a suffocating
amount of student loans,

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:50.250
if they could afford to support
you during the recession

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:52.590
so you didn't rack up credit card debt,

00:06:52.590 --> 00:06:54.300
or if they helped you buy a house

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:56.010
when you were still in your 20s,

00:06:56.010 --> 00:06:59.280
you're probably ahead of
them when they were your age.

00:06:59.280 --> 00:07:01.890
- And that's kind of how it's always been,

00:07:01.890 --> 00:07:05.682
whether you were born in 1987 or 1947.

00:07:05.682 --> 00:07:07.470
- [Julia and Philip] And
that's our Two Cents.

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:10.410
- Before you go, we want
to hear your feedback.

